2026-07-30 ~13:10 PT (arch) — **ADR-038 AMENDMENT A filed: the decision STANDS, the verification claims are corrected, and the error class is named.** ADR-038 (Sep/Oct 2025) replaced ADR-013's maximalism with three-pattern pluralism and asserted per-connector verification — *"Notion spatial: 1 file, 100% operational, 8-dimensional analysis working"*, *"Production-proven: All operational in production codebase"* (§§106-112, 134, 144, 204). **A1 — the DECISION is validated, not weakened**: every connector a user can actually connect has a live spatial path today, and they arrive by *different* patterns, which is exactly what the ADR predicted (Slack ← `integrations/slack/spatial_adapter`, 3 importers, Granular; Calendar ← `mcp/consumer/google_calendar_adapter` via `calendar_integration_router`, Delegated MCP; GitHub ← `github_spatial` + `github_adapter`, full 8-dim; Notion ← `mcp/consumer/notion_adapter`). **A2 — ONE of three citations went stale, and only one**: Slack ✅ holds, Calendar ✅ holds, **Notion ❌ CONTRADICTED** — `intelligence/spatial/notion_spatial` has ZERO importers and ~12 undefined methods (~75% abandoned). **But Notion's spatial CAPABILITY is live** via `mcp/consumer/notion_adapter` (produces SpatialContext/SpatialPosition); what is dead is the specific file the ADR cited as proof. **A3 — THE METHODOLOGICAL FINDING, and the reason this is an amendment rather than a correction: ADR-038 was right about the pattern and wrong about which connector proved it.** The citation didn't go stale because the pattern failed — it went stale because **CORE-MCP-MIGRATION #198 moved the implementation**, replacing per-connector direct-API spatial modules with MCP consumer adapters built on the *same* spatial contract (`BaseSpatialAdapter`/`SpatialContext` in `services/integrations/spatial_adapter.py`), while the ADR kept pointing at the superseded predecessor. **ERROR CLASS: citing an IMPLEMENTATION as evidence for a PATTERN that outlives it.** An implementation is a point-in-time fact with a short half-life; a pattern is a decision. Binding the second to the first means a *successful* migration makes the ADR look falsified — which is precisely what happened: an agent reading §107 against the cold `notion_spatial` file concludes the pattern failed, when in fact it succeeded so thoroughly that the code it was demonstrated on became redundant. **FORWARD RULE adopted for the corpus**: an ADR may cite implementations as *illustration*, never as *proof of continuing validity*; where a live/cold claim is needed it must point at a command that re-derives it rather than freezing a table. **A4 — the amendment deliberately does NOT restate the inventory**; it lives in `spatial-intelligence-layer-map-and-costed-options.md` and is re-derivable via `scripts/reachability-map.py`, with the corpus convention *"if any table disagrees with the command, the command is right"* (CXO's formulation — *"prose can't be re-run; the tool can"* — adopted as convention, not just a note). Also recorded: **a string match on a module name is not an import edge** — CXO and I independently mistook `config_service.py:222`'s feature-flag string `"notion_spatial_mapping"` for an importer within a day of each other. **A5 — closes nothing else**: the committed-theory review has NOT concluded; PPM's roadmap slice, Lead's L4 monitoring-loop estimate, and PM's call on the 10-module cold island remain open, and **spatial deletions remain HELD** (protected representation). The cold island is to be described as *"superseded implementation strategy, retained as prior art"* (CXO's caveat — it holds the only worked examples of place-modeling for four connectors), never as "dead code," since the careless framing would be this ADR's own error run in reverse. Supersedes the two placeholder notices CXO added at the head of ADR-038 on 7/29. — Arch

